D.append(dict(description='American social historian whose studies connect workers, consumers, public power and the built environment. Cohen’s interview on Saving America’s Cities explains her shift from ordinary groups to Ed Logue’s public career, retaining attention to social identities and unequal resources. She interprets urban renewal through changing relations between planning experts and community participation, with costs as well as achievements.',sources=[S('BackStory: A Conversation with Lizabeth Cohen(2020)','https://backstoryradio.org/blog/a-conversation-with-lizabeth-cohen/','Own answers on earlier social history, biography, federal policy and pluralist/participatory urban planning read.'),S('Cambridge UP, Making a New Deal second-edition front matter','https://assets.cambridge.org/97805217/15355/frontmatter/9780521715355_frontmatter.pdf','Explicit original1990, second-edition framing and A Consumers’ Republic2003; publisher text used only for bibliography.',True),S('Lizabeth Cohen, Saving America’s Cities excerpt/interview, Public Seminar1 October2019','https://publicseminar.org/essays/public-seminar-excerpt-and-interview-lizabeth-cohen/','Author excerpt opening and copyright2019; social identity and public-career framing.'),S('Harvard: Lizabeth Cohen current faculty biography','https://history.fas.harvard.edu/people/lizabeth-cohen','Current research-professor role and ongoing coauthored project with Herrick Chapman; positive institutional life evidence.')],works=[W(1990,'Making a New Deal',1),W(2003,'A Consumers’ Republic',1),W(2019,'Saving America’s Cities',2,'Original2019 title/excerpt copyright checked.')],edges=[E('social','Cohen’s move from workers and consumers to a powerful planner retains social history’s attention to class, gender, race and ethnicity.','Own BackStory answer on research design explicitly identifies the continuity and change; no assertion that biography replaces social history.',0),E('urbanhistory','Saving America’s Cities investigates how public redevelopment and residents’ participation changed across postwar cities.','Own BackStory discussion of pluralist and participatory democracy, federal resources and community negotiation; urban renewal is assessed with both costs and achievements.',0)],life=L('living',3,'Current role and project','Positive current Harvard biography and ongoing research.')))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of modern European nationalism and the cultural politics of exclusion. Steven Aschheim reads Mosse’s work as linking national mobilization to bourgeois respectability and masculine stereotypes, while Fallen Soldiers investigates the political remaking of wartime death. Aschheim also questions aspects of this interpretation; cultural analysis does not make Nazi violence inevitable or erase institutional and material forces.',scope='Named substantial reader Steven E. Aschheim’s2023 transcript, especially26:50–30:00 and discussion of Fallen Soldiers, inspected. His critical mediation is explicit; underlying books not read.',sources=[S('Steven E. Aschheim, George L. Mosse: Historian of Fascism, Masculinity, and Nationalism, Columbia Mosse Lecture27 April2023 transcript','https://mosselectures.wisc.edu/2023/04/27/aschheim/','Transcript26:50–30:00 on Fallen Soldiers, Nationalism and Sexuality and Image of Man read, including limits of bourgeois-respectability account. Victoria de Grazia response is separately attributed.'),S('Wisconsin History: George L. Mosse memorial resolution','https://history.wisc.edu/people-main/in-memoriam/','Mosse section dates Nationalism and Sexuality1985,Fallen Soldiers1990,Image of Man1996; explicit death22 January1999.')],works=[W(1985,'Nationalism and Sexuality',1),W(1990,'Fallen Soldiers',1,'Original1990 per faculty memorial; Oxford1991 paperback is a different manifestation.'),W(1996,'The Image of Man',1)],edges=[E('nationalismstudies','Mosse examines nationalism through symbols, respectable conduct and the production of insiders and outsiders.','Aschheim2023 substantial reading26:50–30:00; his qualification of Mosse’s bourgeois account retained.',0),E('gender','Mosse’s history of masculinity treats the normative male body as a component of national belonging and exclusion.','Aschheim’s explicit reading of Nationalism and Sexuality and Image of Man,26:50–30:00; this does not claim complete women’s history coverage.',0),E('memory','Fallen Soldiers investigates the political transformation of wartime death and commemoration.','Aschheim26:50 identifies appropriation of the war experience by the militant right; not automatic causal passage from all war memorials to fascism.',0)],life=L('deceased',1,'Mosse memorial opening','Explicit22 January1999 atMadison.',1999,'1999-01-22')))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of South Asia and empire whose global comparisons retained local and regional foundations. In Binu M. John’s interview Bayly explains modernity as interacting changes across several societies rather than a single Western essence. He also challenges accounts of an all-powerful colonial state by emphasizing Indian intermediaries and the uneven development of British rule. These positions coexist with his explicit refusal to identify only as a global historian.',scope='Binu M. John’s late2006 interview, printed pp187–194 in World History—a Genealogy, read in LOC publisher PDF; complete anthology and Bayly monographs not read.',sources=[S('Binu M. John interviews C.A. Bayly, late2006, in World History—a Genealogy, pp187–194','https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/gdc/gdcebookspublic/20/20/71/53/68/2020715368/2020715368.pdf','Own answers pp189–193 on Indian cooperation, multiple origins of modernity, and local/global scales inspected; interview date distinct from anthology publication.'),S('Tony Ballantyne, Putting the nation in its place?, in Transnational Ties(2008), chapter2','https://press.anu.edu.au/downloads/press/p97101/mobile/ch02.html','Named reader’s Bayly discussion and critical framing read; bibliographic references date Rulers Townsmen and Bazaars1983 and Birth Modern World2004. Reader mediation, not own-book reading.'),S('Cambridge: Professor Sir Christopher Bayly1945–2015','https://www.cam.ac.uk/news/professor-sir-christopher-bayly-1945-2015','Institutional obituary and book bibliography, explicitly deceased2015.')],works=[W(1983,'Rulers, Townsmen and Bazaars',1,'Original1983 bibliography reference in Ballantyne.'),W(2004,'The Birth of the Modern World',1),W(2006,'Interview on local, imperial and global history',0,'Interview conducted late2006 per Binu John introduction; writing/recording event, not anthology publication date.')],edges=[E('global','Bayly’s global comparison traces interacting forms of modernity while retaining local and regional research.','Own late2006 interview pp190–193; he explicitly refuses exclusive identification as a global historian.',0),E('post','Bayly challenges depictions of an omnipotent colonial state and argues that empire depended on Indian intermediaries and uneven cooperation.','Own interview pp189–190; critical engagement with colonial/postcolonial historiography, not membership in a unified postcolonial school.',0)],life=L('deceased',2,'Obituary title and opening','Institution explicitly records death2015; exact-day discrepancy not adjudicated here.',2015)))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of slavery and emancipation who emphasized geographical variation, change over time and enslaved people’s agency. Berlin’s1999 teaching forum sets North American slavery within Atlantic migration and cultural transformation. His Freedmen and Southern Society Project made emancipation documents available through collaborative editing; these collective credits remain distinct from his monographs.',sources=[S('Ira Berlin, Opening Statement, Forum on Slavery1 October1999','https://historymatters.gmu.edu/talkinghistory/SlaveryForum/SlaveryForum-1999-October.html','Own opening statement points1–5 read: Atlantic/global frame, long chronology, northern slavery, negotiation under coercion, cultural exchange. Replies are not attributed to Berlin.'),S('National Endowment for the Humanities, statement on death of Ira Berlin8 June2018','https://www.neh.gov/news/press-release/2018-06-08','Institutional obituary dates Slaves Without Masters1974,Many Thousands Gone1998,Generations of Captivity2003; identifies collaborative Freedmen/Southern Society Project.')],works=[W(1974,'Slaves Without Masters',1),W(1998,'Many Thousands Gone',1),W(2003,'Generations of Captivity',1)],edges=[E('black','Berlin’s slavery history emphasizes variation across time and region and agency exercised within coercive relations.','Own1999 opening points2–4; negotiation does not imply equality between enslaved people and owners.',0),E('atlantic','Berlin frames slavery through Atlantic migration and cultural exchange linking Africa, Europe and the Americas.','Own opening points1 and5; comparative/worldwide framing does not collapse distinct slave societies.',0)],life=L('deceased',1,'Dated institutional death statement','NEH mourns death in2018.',2018)))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of early America whose work connects political ideas, population movement and Atlantic processes. His presidential address treats early America as inseparable from European and African histories and argues for integrating specialized inquiries. Bailyn’s account of revolutionary ideas remains an interpretive intervention; neither institutional recognition nor later publication dates establish living status or sole authorship of Atlantic history.',sources=[S('Bernard Bailyn, The Challenge of Modern Historiography, AHA address1981, AHR87(1982), pp1–24','https://www.historians.org/presidential-address/bernard-bailyn/','Own opening and ensuing population/Atlantic discussion inspected; addressDecember1981 distinct from February1982 publication.'),S('Harvard Atlantic History: Bernard Bailyn bibliography','https://atlantic.fas.harvard.edu/people/bernard-bailyn','Bibliographic list dates Ideological Origins1967,Voyagers1986,Atlantic History2005; historical present-tense bio is stale and not life evidence.',True),S('Harvard History: Bernard Bailyn death announcement','https://history.fas.harvard.edu/people/bernard-bailyn-0','Explicit death7 August2020; organizes Atlantic seminar1995–2010.'),S('Harvard Gazette faculty memorial minute: Bernard Bailyn,25 March2021','https://news.harvard.edu/gazette/story/2021/03/bernard-bailyn-97/','Named faculty memorial’s account of pamphlets and Revolutionary ideology read; establishes interpretive contribution rather than bibliographic metadata alone.')],works=[W(1967,'The Ideological Origins of the American Revolution',1),W(1986,'Voyagers to the West',1),W(2005,'Atlantic History: Concept and Contours',1)],edges=[E('atlantic','Bailyn’s population research locates early America within connections among Europe,Africa and the Americas.','Own1981/1982 address opening; continental North America not a self-contained unit and process differs from a static Atlantic culture.',0),E('political','The Ideological Origins uses revolutionary pamphlets to reconstruct historically consequential fears about power and liberty.','Faculty memorial’s substantive account of Bailyn’s interpretation, not an assertion that ideological explanation exhausted the Revolution.',3)],life=L('deceased',2,'Opening institutional announcement','Explicit7 August2020.',2020,'2020-08-07')))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of books and eighteenth-century French culture who connects the production and circulation of texts with readers’ meanings. Carla Hesse’s1999 account explains how archives of publishing and clandestine literature intersected with Darnton’s collaboration with Clifford Geertz. His anthropological interpretations remain readings of situated symbolic practices; the archive does not give unmediated access to every reader or a single cause of revolution.',sources=[S('Carla Hesse, Robert Darnton, AHA presidential biography1999','https://www.historians.org/person/robert-darnton/','Named reader’s substantive biography, sections on publishing archives, literary underground and Geertz collaboration inspected; dates books1982,1984,1995.'),S('La fin des Lumières, fifty years later: Robert Darnton interview, Annales historiques de la Révolution française2018/1, pp197ff','https://shs.cairn.info/journal-annales-historiques-de-la-revolution-francaise-2018-1-page-197?lang=en','Indexed author answer52 on20-year Geertz seminar and anthropological Cat Massacre read; direct endpoint403. This limited indexed passage supports acknowledged methodological uptake only.'),S('Harvard Early Modern World Fall Aperitivo15 September2026','https://history.fas.harvard.edu/event/harvard-early-modern-world-fall-aperitivo','Positive2026 scheduled Darnton talk on Revolution,Information and Writers; recent institutional activity evidence.')],works=[W(1982,'The Literary Underground of the Old Regime',0),W(1984,'The Great Cat Massacre',0),W(1995,'The Forbidden Best-Sellers of Pre-Revolutionary France',0)],edges=[E('culture','Darnton reads symbolic practices of eighteenth-century French life through a historically situated anthropological approach.','Own2018 answer52 acknowledges Geertz’s seminar influence and calls Cat Massacre anthropological; selected indexed passage, not complete interview.',1),E('intellectualhistory','Publishing and clandestine-book archives connect intellectual production to circulation and audiences in pre-revolutionary France.','Carla Hesse’s1999 substantial account of the publishing archive and literary underground; circulation does not by itself prove every reader’s response.',0)],life=L('living',2,'15 September2026 event','Positive current institutional talk listing; event participation itself not independently observed.')))
D.append(dict(description='Historian of American social organization and democracy. Northwestern’s archival biography identifies The Search for Order as an interpretation of the transition from local communities toward bureaucratic organization. Ashley Woodiwiss’s substantial reading of Self-Rule emphasizes tensions between participatory self-government, centralization and hierarchy, while retaining the racial and gender exclusions of earlier democracy. These are historically argued models, not inevitable stages.',scope='Northwestern archival biography and Ashley Woodiwiss’s1996 Books & Culture review read; monographs not independently read. Reader’s account of Self-Rule is explicitly mediated.',sources=[S('Northwestern University Archives: Robert H. Wiebe biography','https://findingaids.library.northwestern.edu/agents/people/1122','Biography identifies works, dates and Search for Order argument; explicit death10 December2000.'),S('Ashley Woodiwiss, Slouching Toward November, Books & Culture May/June1996 p24','https://www.christianitytoday.com/1996/05/slouching-toward-november/','Named substantive reader’s Self-Rule sections on work, local democracy, gender/race exclusions, centralization and hierarchy inspected. De Gruchy’s contrasting argument is not Wiebe’s.')],works=[W(1962,'Businessmen and Reform',0),W(1967,'The Search for Order',0),W(1995,'Self-Rule',0)],edges=[E('political','Self-Rule interprets American democracy through participatory institutions and the constraints of centralization and hierarchy.','Ashley Woodiwiss1996 reading; earlier white-male democracy’s exclusions remain explicit rather than an idealized universal democracy.',1),E('social','Wiebe connects changing work relations and social hierarchies to the possibilities of democratic self-government.','Woodiwiss’s Self-Rule discussion of transformed work, voluntary groups and the modern class system; substantive social interpretation, not merely a department label.',1)],life=L('deceased',0,'Final biographical paragraph','Explicit10 December2000.',2000,'2000-12-10')))
